\label{ch:multi-level-ir}

Modern compilation targets a diverse landscape of hardware---CPUs, GPUs, TPUs, FPGAs, quantum processors---and serves an increasingly diverse set of domains---machine learning, graphics, quantum computing, database query processing. A single IR cannot adequately serve all these needs. \emph{Multi-level IRs} address this challenge by providing a framework for defining multiple IR \emph{dialects}, each suited to a particular domain or abstraction level, with systematic lowering between them. The MLIR project~\cite{lattner2021} is the most prominent realization of this vision.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 62.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Motivation: Different Abstractions for Different Domains}\label{sec:multi-level-motivation}

The fundamental problem is that different domains require different abstractions.

\subsection{The Abstraction Gap}\label{sec:abstraction-gap}

\begin{example}[Tensor computation]\label{ex:tensor-gap}
A machine learning program operates on \emph{tensors}: multi-dimensional arrays with operations like matrix multiplication, convolution, and reduction. These operations have rich algebraic properties (associativity, distributivity) that enable high-level optimizations (fusion, tiling, parallelization).

When lowered to a traditional IR (e.g., LLVM), these properties are lost: a matrix multiplication becomes a triple-nested loop, and the compiler must rediscover the structure from the loop nest.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Quantum computation]\label{ex:quantum-gap}
A quantum program operates on \emph{qubits} and \emph{quantum gates}: unitary transformations on quantum states. These operations have properties (commutativity of certain gates, circuit identities) that enable quantum-specific optimizations (gate cancellation, circuit simplification).

When lowered to a traditional IR, these properties are completely lost.
\end{example}

\subsection{The Multi-Level Solution}\label{sec:multi-level-solution}

The multi-level IR approach maintains multiple levels of abstraction, each with its own operations, types, and semantics. The program is progressively lowered from high-level abstractions to low-level machine operations, with each level preserving the structure relevant to that level.

