\section{MLIR: Dialects and Lowering}\label{sec:mlir}

MLIR (Multi-Level Intermediate Representation) is a framework for building compilers that use multiple IR levels. It provides the infrastructure for defining dialects, converting between them, and applying optimizations at each level.

\subsection{Dialects}\label{sec:mlir-dialects}

\begin{definition}[Dialect]\label{def:dialect}
A \emph{dialect} in MLIR is a collection of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Operations.} The operations defined by the dialect (e.g., \texttt{tensor.matmul}, \texttt{affine.for}).
  \item \textbf{Types.} The types used by the dialect (e.g., \texttt{tensor<4x4xf32>}, \texttt{memref<4x4xf32>}).
  \item \textbf{Attributes.} Compile-time constant values (e.g., integer constants, dense elements).
  \item \textbf{Semantics.} The meaning of each operation, specified informally or formally.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Example: The Affine Dialect}\label{sec:affine-dialect}

The \texttt{affine} dialect represents loop nests with affine (linear) bounds and access patterns:

\begin{verbatim}
  affine.for %i = 0 to %N {
    affine.for %j = 0 to %M {
      %val = affine.load %A[%i + %j] : memref<100xf32>
      affine.store %val, %B[%i * 2 + %j] : memref<200xf32>
    }
  }
\end{verbatim}

The affine dialect enables polyhedral optimizations (loop interchange, fusion, tiling, parallelization) because the loop bounds and array accesses are affine functions of the loop variables.

\subsection{Example: The Linalg Dialect}\label{sec:linalg-dialect}

The \texttt{linalg} dialect represents linear algebra operations in a generic form:

\begin{verbatim}
  %C = linalg.matmul ins(%A, %B : tensor<4x8xf32>, tensor<8x16xf32>)
                     outs(%C_init : tensor<4x16xf32>) -> tensor<4x16xf32>
\end{verbatim}

The \texttt{linalg} dialect supports operations like matrix multiplication, convolution, and reduction, with generic indexing maps that enable pattern-based optimization.

\subsection{Lowering Between Dialects}\label{sec:mlir-lowering}

Lowering converts operations from one dialect to another, typically from a higher-level dialect to a lower-level one:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before lowering (tensor dialect):
  %C = tensor.matmul %A, %B : tensor<4x8xf32>, tensor<8x16xf32> -> tensor<4x16xf32>

  // After lowering (linalg dialect):
  %C = linalg.matmul ins(%A, %B) outs(%C_init) : tensor<4x8xf32>, tensor<8x16xf32> -> tensor<4x16xf32>

  // After further lowering (scf + memref dialect):
  %C_mem = bufferization.to_memref %C_init
  scf.for %i = 0 to 4 {
    scf.for %j = 0 to 16 {
      scf.for %k = 0 to 8 {
        %a = memref.load %A[%i, %k]
        %b = memref.load %B[%k, %j]
        %c = memref.load %C_mem[%i, %j]
        %prod = arith.mulf %a, %b
        %sum = arith.addf %c, %prod
        memref.store %sum, %C_mem[%i, %j]
      }
    }
  }
\end{verbatim}

