\section{High-Level to Low-Level IR Transitions}\label{sec:transitions}

The transition from high-level to low-level IR involves several key transformations.

\subsection{Bufferization}\label{sec:bufferization}

\emph{Bufferization} converts tensor (value semantics) operations to memref (reference semantics) operations:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before (tensor):
  %t1 = tensor.generate { ... } : tensor<100xf32>
  %t2 = tensor.extract_slice %t1[0][50] : tensor<100xf32> to tensor<50xf32>

  // After (memref):
  %m1 = memref.alloc() : memref<100xf32>
  // ... fill %m1 ...
  %m2 = memref.subview %m1[0][50] : memref<100xf32> to memref<50xf32>
\end{verbatim}

Bufferization determines where to allocate memory and how to manage lifetimes, a critical step for efficient execution.

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Choosing a Copy for a Tensor Update During Bufferization}

A functional tensor update returns a new tensor and leaves the
old value available to its remaining users. During bufferization
it can become an in-place store only if overwriting the buffer
cannot be observed through the old value. Consider a straight-line
region, a destination tensor with a known mutable backing buffer,
and an update of a known portion of that buffer. Its lowering
must read the update's inputs before any that might be clobbered
are overwritten. Require complete alias and later-read information
for reuse; unknown reads, escaped views, and overlapping old-value
returns are conservatively treated as conflicts.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Update of tensor $x$, written range $W$, later old-value users $U$
\State $b\gets$ backing buffer of $x$
\State $reuse\gets$ mutable buffer and safe update evaluation
\ForAll{$u\in U$}
  \If{the old-version read of $u$ may overlap $W$}
    \State $reuse\gets\mathrm{false}$ \Comment{Include aliased views}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\If{not $reuse$}
  \State $b\gets$ fresh buffer with the same shape as $x$
  \State copy all elements of $x$ into $b$ \Comment{Keep untouched elements}
\EndIf
\State emit the update on $b$ from the original operands
\State \Return $b$ as the new tensor's storage
\end{sudocode}

Let $x=[3,5,8]$ and let $y$ update element 1 of $x$ to 9,
using zero-based indices. A later read of element 1 through a
view of the old $x$ must return 5. Reusing $x$ would make that
read return 9, so the procedure copies and gives $y=[3,9,8]$
in a new buffer. If the only later old-value read is instead
element 0, it still returns 3 after an in-place update.

The invariant is observational: every remaining old-version
read obtains the same data as in the tensor dialect. A copy is
linear in the tensor size; the decision itself examines each
later user once in this simplified region. For loops or branches,
``later'' and ``old version'' require control-flow reasoning.
Keeping tensor alias relations explicit until bufferization makes
it possible to check this obligation before the rewrite turns a
functional update into a store.

\subsection{Loop Lowering}\label{sec:loop-lowering}

High-level loop constructs are lowered to lower-level loops:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before (affine):
  affine.for %i = 0 to %N step 1 {
    affine.load %A[%i * 2 + 1]
  }

  // After (scf):
  scf.for %i = %c0 to %N step %c1 {
    %idx = arith.muli %i, %c2
    %idx2 = arith.addi %idx, %c1
    memref.load %A[%idx2]
  }
\end{verbatim}

The affine dialect's structure (affine bounds and accesses) is lost in the scf dialect, but the optimizations enabled by the affine structure (tiling, parallelization) have already been applied.

\subsection{Type Lowering}\label{sec:type-lowering}

High-level types are lowered to low-level types:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before:
  tensor<4x4xf32>   // 4x4 tensor of 32-bit floats

  // After:
  memref<4x4xf32>   // 4x4 memref of 32-bit floats

  // After further lowering:
  !llvm.ptr          // pointer to float array (flattened)
\end{verbatim}

