\section{Preserving Structure During Lowering}\label{sec:preserving-structure}

A key challenge in multi-level IRs is preserving high-level structure during lowering, so that optimizations can be applied at the appropriate level.

\subsection{The Structure Preservation Problem}\label{sec:structure-problem}

When a high-level operation is lowered to a sequence of low-level operations, the high-level structure is lost. For example, a matrix multiplication lowered to a triple loop is no longer recognizable as a matrix multiplication.

\subsection{Solutions}\label{sec:structure-solutions}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Gradual lowering.} Lower through multiple intermediate levels, each preserving some structure.
  \item \textbf{Selective lowering.} Lower only the parts of the program that need to be lowered; keep high-level operations where possible.
  \item \textbf{Structure tagging.} Add metadata to low-level operations indicating their high-level origin.
  \item \textbf{Bidirectional conversion.} Allow raising low-level operations back to high-level ones (e.g., recognizing loop nests as matrix multiplications).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{One-Shot vs. Incremental Lowering}\label{sec:oneshot-vs-incremental}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{One-shot lowering.} Convert the entire program from one dialect to another in a single pass. Simple but loses structure.
  \item \textbf{Incremental lowering.} Convert one operation (or small group) at a time, allowing interleaved optimization. Preserves structure but is more complex.
\end{itemize}

MLIR supports both approaches, with the \texttt{--convert-X-to-Y} passes providing one-shot lowering and the dialect conversion infrastructure supporting incremental lowering.

