\section{Cross-Dialect Optimization}\label{sec:cross-dialect}

One of the most powerful features of multi-level IRs is the ability to perform optimizations that span multiple dialects.

\subsection{Fusion Across Dialects}\label{sec:cross-fusion}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before fusion (linalg dialect):
  %B = linalg.matmul ins(%A, %W1) outs(%B_init) // matmul
  %C = linalg.generic { ... } ins(%B) outs(%C_init) // element-wise op on result

  // After fusion:
  %C = linalg.generic { ... } ins(%A, %W1) outs(%C_init) {
    // fused: compute matmul element and apply element-wise op in one pass
  }
\end{verbatim}

The fusion optimization combines two operations into one, reducing memory traffic. This optimization requires understanding both the \texttt{linalg.matmul} and \texttt{linalg.generic} operations.

\subsection{Tiling and Parallelization}\label{sec:cross-tiling}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before tiling (affine dialect):
  affine.for %i = 0 to %N {
    affine.for %j = 0 to %M {
      %v = affine.load %A[%i, %j]
      affine.store %v, %B[%i, %j]
    }
  }

  // After tiling:
  affine.for %i0 = 0 to %N step 32 {
    affine.for %j0 = 0 to %M step 32 {
      affine.for %i1 = %i0 to min(%i0 + 32, %N) {
        affine.for %j1 = %j0 to min(%j0 + 32, %M) {
          %v = affine.load %A[%i1, %j1]
          affine.store %v, %B[%i1, %j1]
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // After parallelization:
  affine.parallel (%i0, %j0) = (0, 0) to (%N, %M) step (32, 32) {
    // tile body (parallelized)
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Cross-Dialect Analysis}\label{sec:cross-analysis}

Cross-dialect analyses combine information from multiple levels:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Memory analysis.} Combine tensor-level analysis (what data is accessed) with memref-level analysis (where it is stored).
  \item \textbf{Alias analysis.} Combine high-level type information (tensors don't alias) with low-level pointer analysis.
  \item \textbf{Effect analysis.} Combine high-level effect annotations (pure tensor operations) with low-level effects (memory writes).
\end{itemize}

