\begin{insight}
The multi-level IR approach represents a paradigm shift in compiler design. Instead of a single IR that tries to serve all purposes, a multi-level IR provides a spectrum of abstractions, each suited to a particular kind of optimization. The key insight is that structure is valuable: a matrix multiplication expressed as \texttt{matmul} can be optimized in ways that a triple loop cannot. By preserving structure as long as possible (through gradual lowering), the compiler can apply the most powerful optimizations at the highest possible level.
\end{insight}

\begin{historical}
The multi-level IR concept was realized in the MLIR project, initiated by Chris Lattner at Google in 2019~\cite{lattner2021}. MLIR grew out of the limitations of LLVM IR for machine learning workloads: LLVM's type system and operations were too low-level to express tensor computations efficiently. MLIR's dialect system allows new abstractions to be added without modifying the core infrastructure. The project has been adopted by LLVM, TensorFlow, PyTorch, and many other projects, demonstrating the value of the multi-level approach. The concept of multi-level IRs extends beyond ML: quantum computing compilers (QIRO, QuiRL), database systems (for query optimization), and graphics compilers (for shader optimization) all benefit from domain-specific abstractions that can be progressively lowered to machine code.
\end{historical}

