\section{Advanced MLIR Dialects and Cross-Dialect Optimization}\label{sec:mlir-advanced}

\subsection{Defining Custom Dialects}\label{sec:custom-dialects}

MLIR's extensibility comes from its ability to define custom dialects. Each dialect encapsulates a level of abstraction with its own operations, types, and semantics.

\begin{definition}[Dialect Definition]\label{def:dialect-def}
A dialect in MLIR consists of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Operations.} Defined by their name, operands, results, attributes, and regions.
  \item \textbf{Types.} Custom types with parsing, printing, and verification.
  \item \textbf{Attributes.} Compile-time constant values specific to the dialect.
  \item \textbf{Interfaces.} Contracts that operations can implement (e.g., \texttt{SideEffect}, \texttt{InferType}).
  \item \textbf{Traits.} Static properties of operations (e.g., \texttt{Commutative}, \texttt{ConstantLike}).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Example: Defining a custom dialect for image processing
  def ImageDialect : Dialect {
    let name = "img";
    let summary = "Image processing operations";
  }

  // Operations:
  def Img_Convolve : Op<ImageDialect, "convolve"> {
    let arguments = (ins
      TensorType:$input,
      TensorType:$kernel
    );
    let results = (outs TensorType:$output);
    let assemblyFormat = "$input `,` $kernel attr-dict `:` type($input)";
  }

  // Usage:
  %result = img.convolve %image, %kernel : tensor<256x256xf32>, tensor<3x3xf32>
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Dialect Conversion Infrastructure}\label{sec:dialect-conversion}

MLIR provides a dialect conversion framework for systematically lowering operations from one dialect to another:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Dialect Conversion}\label{alg:dialect-conversion}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{ConvertDialect}{$\mathrm{module}, \mathrm{source\_dialect}, \mathrm{target\_dialect}$}
  \State $\mathrm{converter} \gets \mathrm{ConversionPatternRewriter}()$
  \For{each operation $op$ in $\mathrm{module}$}
    \If{$op.\mathrm{dialect} == \mathrm{source\_dialect}$}
      \State $\mathrm{pattern} \gets \mathrm{find\_pattern}(op, \mathrm{target\_dialect})$
      \If{$\mathrm{pattern} \neq \mathrm{null}$}
        \State $\mathrm{converter}.\mathrm{replaceOp}(op, \mathrm{pattern}.\mathrm{rewrite}(op))$
      \Else
        \State $\mathrm{converter}.\mathrm{legalize}(op)$ \Comment{Mark as legal in target}
      \EndIf
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $\mathrm{module}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Cross-Dialect Optimization Patterns}\label{sec:cross-dialect-patterns}

Cross-dialect optimizations exploit information from multiple abstraction levels:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Pattern 1: Tensor-level fusion followed by loop-level tiling
  // Step 1: Fuse matmul + bias + relu at the tensor level
  %fused = linalg.fused_matmul_bias_relu %A, %B, %bias
           : tensor<4x8xf32>, tensor<8x16xf32>, tensor<16xf32>
           -> tensor<4x16xf32>

  // Step 2: Lower to loops with tiling
  scf.for %i = 0 to 4 step 2 {
    scf.for %j = 0 to 16 step 4 {
      scf.for %k = 0 to 8 step 2 {
        // Tiled matmul with fused bias and relu
        // Each tile fits in cache
      }
    }
  }

  // Step 3: Vectorize the innermost loops
  // The tile size is chosen to match SIMD register width
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{The Affine Dialect: Polyhedral Optimization}\label{sec:affine-polyhedral}

The affine dialect enables polyhedral optimization by restricting loop bounds and array accesses to affine functions of loop variables:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Affine dialect: all bounds and accesses are affine
  affine.for %i = 0 to %N {
    affine.for %j = %i + 1 to %M {
      %v = affine.load %A[2 * %i + %j] : memref<100xf32>
      affine.store %v, %B[%i + %j] : memref<200xf32>
    }
  }

  // Polyhedral transformations enabled:
  // 1. Loop interchange: swap %i and %j loops
  // 2. Loop fusion: merge two loop nests with same bounds
  // 3. Tiling: split loops into outer (tile) and inner (element) loops
  // 4. Parallelization: mark outer loops as parallel
  // 5. Skewing: apply affine transformation to iteration space
\end{verbatim}

