\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-62}

Multi-level IRs address the fundamental challenge of compiling diverse programs to diverse hardware. By providing a framework for defining domain-specific dialects and systematically lowering between them, multi-level IRs preserve high-level structure while enabling low-level optimization. The MLIR project has demonstrated the practical viability of this approach, with applications in machine learning, quantum computing, and beyond.

The key design principles are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Dialects.} Each level of abstraction is a dialect with its own operations, types, and semantics.
  \item \textbf{Gradual lowering.} Programs are lowered through multiple levels, preserving structure at each level.
  \item \textbf{Cross-dialect optimization.} Optimizations can operate at the most appropriate level, using information from multiple dialects.
\end{itemize}

For further reading, we recommend the MLIR overview paper~\cite{lattner2021}, the IREE documentation~\cite{iree2024}, and the survey of multi-level IRs by Lattner et al.
